-**DMT (N,N-Dimethyltryptamine) — Plant Sources, Tradition, Clinical Research, Legal Status (2026)**
-
-SOURCES (plants):
-- Richest source: Mimosa tenuiflora (syn. M. hostilis, jurema), NE Brazil tree — root bark 0.5–1.7% DMT dry weight (some analyses ~2%), stem bark ~0.3%.
-- Psychotria viridis (chacruna), Amazon shrub — leaves 0.1–0.66% DMT (varies by plant and time of day); the DMT admixture in ayahuasca.
-- Other ayahuasca admixture: Diplopterys cabrerana (chaliponga).
-- Acacia spp. (Australasian/African): bark/leaves/seeds; Phalaris grasses (trace); Desmodium, Virola (bark/resin). Dozens of species across legumes, grasses, trees on nearly every continent.
-- Anadenanthera peregrina/colubrina (yopo/vilca snuffs): primary active is bufotenine, not DMT.
-- Ayahuasca chemistry: DMT alone is orally inactive — gut MAO enzyme degrades it. Brew combines DMT plant (P. viridis) + Banisteriopsis caapi vine, whose β-carbolines (harmine, harmaline, tetrahydroharmine) are MAO inhibitors making DMT orally active. Analysis: ~1.13 mg DMT/g brew (0.53–1.86 range); harmine ~2.83 mg/g.
-- Endogenous: DMT produced in humans — detected in blood (51 pg/ml to 55 ng/ml), urine (0.16–19 ng/ml), CSF; pathway tryptophan → tryptamine → DMT via INMT enzyme; INMT in cerebral cortex, choroid plexus, pineal gland. 2019 rat study: both enzymes in same neurons, concentrations comparable to serotonin. Strassman's "pineal flood at death" (The Spirit Molecule, 2001) remains speculative/unproven.
-- Other animals: mammals, marine sponges, tunicates, frogs. Synthetic DMT is molecularly identical.
-
-TRADITIONAL USE:
-- Ayahuasca (Quechua: aya = soul/spirit, huasca = vine → "vine of the soul"), Amazon/Orinoco basins. Uses: healing, spiritual communion, diagnosis/divination, initiation, communal cohesion. Names: yagé, caapi, natem, hoasca, oni xuma.
-- 72+ documented Indigenous groups: Shipibo-Conibo, Quechua, Asháninka, Shuar, Huitoto/Tukano, Kichwa, Yawanawá, Huni Kuin. Healers: curanderos/vegetalistas/ayahuasqueros; icaros sacred songs; apprenticeship + dieta.
-- Archaeology: ~1,000 CE Bolivian cave ritual bundle with harmine + DMT traces; Ecuador ceremonial cup with ayahuasca traces (claimed ~2,500 yrs). Oral histories claim much older; some anthropologists argue current form spread within last few centuries via missionaries + rubber trade (1879–1912 boom merged traditions).
-- Snuff tradition (yopo/vilca, Anadenanthera) predates ayahuasca; ornate snuff trays/spatulas found in pre-Columbian Andes (Chavín era).
-- Colonial suppression; documented by Richard Spruce (1851), ethnobotany by Richard Evans Schultes (1940s–50s).
-- Brazil syncretic religions: Santo Daime (Raimundo Irineu Serra, 1930s), União do Vegetal — made it legally defensible via RFRA in US.
-- Globalization since ~1990s: retreat tourism, diaspora ceremonies.
-
-CLINICAL RESEARCH:
-- Strassman 1990s (U. New Mexico): ~400 IV DMT-fumarate sessions; first modern human research; source of "spirit molecule" mystique.
-- Mechanism: 5-HT2A agonist (also 5-HT1A, sigma-1, TAARs); promotes neuroplasticity; brief experience (IV ~30 min, inhaled 5–20 min vs ayahuasca 4–6 h).
-- Phase IIa (Nature Medicine, Feb 17 2026): SPL026 (DMT fumarate) IV 21.5 mg over ~10 min, n=34 (17/17), moderate–severe MDD/TRD, London + Liverpool. Primary endpoint met — significantly greater MADRS improvement at 2 weeks vs placebo; effects within 1 week, persisting to 3 months; remission ~30% vs ~10% placebo; no serious AEs (transient BP/HR rise). Lead: David Erritzoe, Imperial College London; trial from Small Pharma (→Cybin UK).
-- Systematic review (Prog. Neuro-Psychopharmacol. Biol. Psychiatry, Jul 2025): favorable safety across 5 early-phase trials (IV, inhaled, oral, intranasal); dose-dependent CV effects (SBP up to +25.7%); no SAEs; manageable psychotomimetic effects.
-- Pipeline: Imperial designing vaporized-DMT trial (no IV infrastructure, dosing consistency challenge); Helus Pharma (took over SPL026) won't advance IV form, pivoting to HLP004 (novel serotonergic agonist, GAD Phase 2 data expected Q1 2026). 5-MeO-DMT (separate compound, from Bufo alvarius toad) — GH Research GH001 Phase 2b (2025) −15.5 MADRS vs placebo in TRD.
-- Context: esketamine (Spravato) FDA-approved 2019; psilocybin (COMP360) Phase 3, FDA decision late 2026, Breakthrough designation; MDMA declined by FDA 2024 (trial-design/ethics concerns). FDA finalized first psychedelic clinical-trial guidance (July 2026) and set Sept 14, 2026 public hearing on therapeutic psychedelics.
-- Open questions: does the subjective experience drive benefit vs. neurobiological effect alone? Does removing purging (ayahuasca vomiting) lose anything? Trial evidence suggests no outcome difference between purgers/non-purgers.
-
-LEGAL STATUS:
-- US federal: Schedule I under CSA (since 1970/71) — no accepted medical use, high abuse potential; felonies under 21 U.S.C. §844/§841. DEA fact sheet active. Endogenous-DMT defense has never been accepted by a court.
-- Only Colorado (Prop 122, 2022) decriminalized personal possession (21+); no DMT healing centers licensed yet — advisory board addressing DMT in 2026 cycle.
-- Oregon Measure 109 covers psilocybin only; DMT not included. California: Schedule I (H&S §11054); SB 751 (2025) plant-medicine framework bill — DMT inclusion uncertain. City-level "lowest priority" resolutions do not make it legal (federal enforcement possible).
-- RFRA religious exemptions: UDV (Gonzales v. O Centro, unanimous SCOTUS 2006) and Santo Daime protected for ayahuasca sacrament; DEA denied Church of the Living Tree and Genesis 1:29.
-- International: Schedule I of UN 1971 Convention on Psychotropic Substances → strictest tier in most signatory countries. UK: Class A (MDA 1971; possession ≤7 yrs, supply life). Portugal: personal possession decriminalized (2001). Netherlands: List I hard drug; small-possession effectively decriminalized, ceremonies operate. Brazil/Peru: ayahuasca recognized as cultural/national heritage; church use legal.
-- Plants themselves aren't scheduled, but extraction/concentration/brewing with intent is prosecutable. 5-MeO-DMT added to Schedule I Jan 19, 2011. 4-AcO-DMT not explicitly listed but Federal Analogue Act (1986) applies if intended for human consumption.
-- Schedule I status is itself a research barrier: investigators need both FDA approval and separate DEA registration.
-**DMT analogs / research chemicals — US legal status**
-- Federal Analogue Act (1986): any substance "substantially similar" in structure/effect to a Schedule I/II drug is treated as Schedule I if intended for human consumption. No genuinely legal for-consumption DMT-type RC exists federally.
-- Explicitly scheduled tryptamines (21 CFR §1308.11): DMT, DET, etryptamine, psilocin, psilocybin; 5-MeO-DMT (Jan 2011); 5-MeO-DiPT ("Foxy"); AMT, DPT; 5 more added Jan 2022 (4-OH-DiPT, 5-MeO-AMT, 5-MeO-MiPT, 5-MeO-DET, DiPT).
-- Unscheduled-by-name but Analogue-Act-exposed: 4-AcO-DMT (psilacetin — direct psilocin prodrug, highest exposure), 4-HO-MET, 4-HO-MiPT, 5-MeO-DALT, 5-MeO-DPT, 4-AcO-DET. Legal only as non-human research reagents; human-consumption intent = Schedule I prosecution.
-- State law closes the gray zone: Alabama (4-AcO-DMT, 2014), SD/WV (4-HO-MET), FL/VA and others explicitly schedule tryptamines.
-- "Not for human consumption" label is a defense only if truthful; DEA prosecutes vendors whose actual traffic is human use.
-- Only US carve-outs: state decriminalization of natural compounds (CO personal DMT/psilocybin 21+; OR psilocybin services only) — nothing covers synthetic analogs.
-- Internationally: only DMT, DET, etryptamine, psilocin, psilocybin are UN 1971 Convention-controlled; some analogs genuinely legal in NL/Czech Republic (compound-specific lists) but not the US.
-**Colorado (Prop 122 / SB 23-290) — legal routes to DMT & psilocybin**
-- Framework: Natural Medicine Health Act (Nov 2022, ~53%) + SB 23-290. Decriminalizes personal use of 5 natural medicines (psilocybin, psilocin, DMT, ibogaine, mescaline excl. peyote) for adults 21+; builds licensed healing-center program.
-- Psilocybin legal routes: (1) personal — possess, cultivate (secured ~12'x12'), use, process, gift; selling illegal, no retail/dispensaries/mail order. (2) licensed healing centers — payment allowed, operating since 2025; 34 licensed by Feb 2026 (9 standard + 25 micro-tier, 18 pending); no diagnosis/referral/residency required (21+ ID + intake screening); on-site supervised use only, no take-home; cost ~$1,500–4,000 out-of-pocket (prep $200–600, dosing $800–2,500, integration $200–600); insurance doesn't cover (federal Schedule I).
-- DMT status: personal-use possession/use/growing/processing/gifting of natural-source DMT state-decriminalized now (C.R.S. 18-18-434(5)(a) — includes grow/process/store/obtain/share). Definition EXCLUDES synthetic DMT and synthetic analogs — only plant-derived covered. No regulated purchase route yet.
-- DMT healing-center route: earliest June 1, 2026, but needs Natural Medicine Advisory Board recommendation (DMT on 2026 cycle; ibogaine already 5–2 board rec Sept 2025) + DORA/DOR rulemaking + licensing → realistically months-years out.
-- Governance: DORA licenses facilitators (lay-facilitator model, no clinical license needed); DOR Natural Medicine Division licenses businesses/centers. Localities can't ban healing centers. Colorado permits administration in healthcare facilities and private residences (broader than Oregon).
-- Federal caveat: all 5 remain Schedule I under CSA; Prop 122 is state law only — federal enforcement possible though untargeted so far. No crossing state lines. Decriminalization also covers gifting but not sale, paid advertising, or commercial promotion.
-- Key differences vs Oregon: CO decriminalizes personal possession + allows home cultivation statewide (OR: neither); CO can expand substances beyond psilocybin (OR is psilocybin-only); OR centers operating since 2023 (more mature).
-**CO "mushroom gifting + delivery fee" model — NOT legal**
-- Verdict: gifting-with-fee is the gray-market workaround the statute was written to defeat; now explicitly criminalized. Providers sell a legal theory, not a legal product.
-- C.R.S. 18-18-434(5)(a) (SB 23-290): sharing is decriminalized only WITHOUT compensation — no "remuneration in exchange for a psychedelic substance," no gifting "as part of a business promotion or commercial activity." An exorbitant delivery fee = remuneration in substance regardless of invoice labels. Natural Medicine Division: commercial sale "remains prohibited, even inside the personal-use environment."
-- HB 26-1325 (signed June 2026, "ibogaine bill"): added criminal language banning advertising natural medicine; using harm-reduction/support services to conduct sales; retail/vendor tables/booths dispensing for remuneration; services or items that include transfer of the medicine; online sales; recurring fees to acquire natural medicine. Directly targets pay-for-support + gift-mushrooms and gift+delivery models. Sponsor Sen. Matt Ball: "Coloradans didn't vote for recreational psilocybin sales."
-- Enforcement examples: Denver's Darren Lyman — paid support/screening + free mushrooms, chocolates, DMT, open since 2023; DOR cease-and-desist 2023; DORA unlicensed-facilitation order 2025; now the test case. Operator disclaimers ("pricing reflects delivery and support services only") are the model's signature, not a shield.
-- Penalties: sale of psilocybin = CO drug felony Level 3→1 by amount (up to 14g: L3, 2–4 yrs/$2K–500K; 14–225g: L2, 4–8 yrs/$3K–750K; 225g+: L1, 8–32 yrs/$5K–1M). Buyer of a paid transfer is on the other side of the same crime.
-- Federal: Schedule I CSA throughout; delivery across state lines/mail = federal trafficking regardless of state gift rules. Only lawful paid route = licensed healing center; only lawful free route = personal cultivation/gifting.
-- Source: Westword, June 11 2026 (HB 26-1325 reporting); manzurilaw.com SB 23-290 analysis.
